00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:34.930
Last question-- who knows what
a machine learning model is?

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:37.450
OK, most of you.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:40.370
So I'll just touch over
this really briefly.

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:43.270
Machine learning is about data,
and taking data, and getting

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:45.250
insights and action.

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:47.290
There's all kinds
of data out there--

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:52.030
images, unstructured text,
relational databases,

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:54.590
sequence information.

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:57.530
And what machine learning
is is taking that data,

00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:00.830
taking an ML algorithm
of various sorts,

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:04.100
and passing through that
data through that algorithm

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:06.660
with a huge set
of infrastructure.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:08.150
And as you pass
that data through,

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:11.190
the machine learning
model is created.

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:13.010
And this model gets
better and better.

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:14.720
The more data, the
better algorithms,

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:16.010
the more infrastructure.

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:19.329
And you use that model to
create insights, automation,

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:21.940
and innovation.

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:26.020
So we all know this
service, web search.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:28.290
Google's been an AI
company and a data company

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:31.770
for 18 years now,
collecting the world's data,

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:34.920
making it accessible
and usable by all.

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:37.080
And we have a lot of
expertise in this area--

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:40.830
large scale data centers,
AI experts, data experts.

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:43.650
And what Google Cloud is is
about bringing that expertise

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:46.780
to you with ML.

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:49.480
This is a nice little story
about how we, internally, have

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:51.820
implemented ML successfully.

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:56.560
Our internal AI team called
Brain, they create ML models--

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:59.380
take a bunch of data,
through an algorithm,

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:01.360
pass it through a huge
scale of infrastructure,

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.160
and create ML models.

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:05.530
But not every single
software developer at Google

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:07.072
is an ML expert.

00:05:07.072 --> 00:05:09.280
So what we've done is, we
went around to our software

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:10.990
developers and
trained them, here's

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:14.320
how you can implement ML
with these existing models.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:17.050
So what this graph is showing
is the number of source code

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:22.120
repositories that our developers
are using the Brain's ML model

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:25.840
to implement successfully
AI into internal services,

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:28.790
external products.

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:32.569
And we're bringing these types
of concepts to Google Cloud.

00:05:32.569 --> 00:05:34.610
On the right, you'll see
a bunch of products that

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:36.100
have integrated and ML and AI.

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:38.600
I'm not going to talk about all
of them, I'll talk about two

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:39.960
of my favorites.

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:42.200
So I'm a father,
I have three kids.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:44.850
And I take a lot of
digital photos of my kids,

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:48.410
a ridiculous amount,
maybe 100,000 in total.

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:50.970
And I had this Mac, and
I had some hard drives,

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:53.840
and I was worried about
storage, so I got Google Photos,

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:55.040
free unlimited storage.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:56.030
Well, that's great.

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:57.446
It's cheap, it's
scalable, I don't

00:05:57.446 --> 00:05:59.030
got to worry about
backing things up.

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:02.150
But really, the innovation
was the search feature.

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:05.002
It has facial recognition
and knows who my kids are.

00:06:05.002 --> 00:06:06.460
I can click on a
picture of my face

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:09.750
in my phone, type in a word
like a beach or a boat,

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:11.930
and now it goes
through 100,000 photos

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:14.970
and finds the exact
photo I'm looking for.

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:17.430
In the past, that would have
taken me 20 minutes to find.

00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:24.650
ML is innovating,
and so could you.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:28.480
So no matter what size your
organization is, no matter what

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:32.990
type of technical staff, there's
different ways to implement ML.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:35.850
And we're going to be
talking about both ways.

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:40.170
The first way is to
use our ML models.

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:41.674
You don't need to
be an ML expert,

00:06:41.674 --> 00:06:43.340
you just need to be
a software developer

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:45.500
or your organization does--

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:48.860
any programming language with
the REST API, simple to use.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:51.870
If you're a CXO out there or
an executive trying to decide,

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:53.540
do I fund the project?

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:57.400
It's very low risk to
take a high quality model,

00:06:57.400 --> 00:06:59.140
have a software
developer use an API,

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:03.120
and implement it, get
started really quickly.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:06.080
And the other route is
to build a custom model.

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:07.940
Maybe you're a domain
expert in your space

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:10.040
or you have access to
the data, and you want

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:11.550
to create your own ML model.

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:15.100
So we have a bunch of tools for
experts, a bunch of hardware

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:17.642
to get you fast and
cheap ML processing.

00:07:17.642 --> 00:07:19.850
And this is where I'm going
to hand it off to Justin,

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:21.849
and he's going to talk
about these in little bit

00:07:21.849 --> 00:07:23.420
more detail.

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:25.119
JUSTIN LAWYER: Thanks, Chris.

00:07:25.119 --> 00:07:26.410
So thanks very much for coming.

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:27.785
Today, we're going
to talk about,

00:07:27.785 --> 00:07:30.410
how do you find the
right tools for your job

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:33.600
as an expert in your field?

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:36.230
So first of all, as
Chris was mentioning,

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:38.570
Google has created a lot
of machine learning models

00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:42.090
for specific applications to
power Google applications.

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:45.020
And so we then have wrapped
REST APIs around these for you

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:46.210
to consume.

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:47.830
We call these the
perception APIs,

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:49.970
because they're typically
around human perception

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:53.360
and how the user interacts
with the application.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:56.940
So quickly going on to
the next slide here,

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:59.240
there are three main
categories of these APIs.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:02.990
One is regarding site, the
other is around conversation,

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:05.254
and the third one
is around language.

00:08:05.254 --> 00:08:07.670
I think it's important to maybe
walk through each of these

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:10.190
really quickly so you can kind
of understand out-of-the-box

00:08:10.190 --> 00:08:12.680
capabilities that you can get
without you doing any coding

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:15.290
other than using a REST
API, and then implement that

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:16.590
into your application.

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:19.302
So first of all, for sight,
say you've got an image,

00:08:19.302 --> 00:08:21.260
and you want to understand
what's in that image

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:22.090
automatically.

00:08:22.090 --> 00:08:24.680
You pass that image
to the Vision API,

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:27.740
and you can understand what the
objects are within that image

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:30.350
automatically, and you can
figure out where the faces are,

00:08:30.350 --> 00:08:32.390
and it can tell
different people.

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:37.070
For the Video Understanding
API, you can pass it the video,

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:39.350
and it can tell scene
transitions, different objects

00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:40.135
within that video.

00:08:40.135 --> 00:08:41.510
You can basically
search and find

00:08:41.510 --> 00:08:44.090
what you're looking for within
the video or across videos

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:46.370
if you're passing
multiple videos.

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:49.455
OCR-- often, people have
documents that they've scanned.

00:08:49.455 --> 00:08:51.080
And you need to kind
of understand what

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:52.320
the heck is in that document.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:54.153
Maybe it's a PDF, maybe
it's a GIF, whatever

00:08:54.153 --> 00:08:55.610
it is, you need to be able to--

00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:56.780
not a GIF, a JPEG--

00:08:56.780 --> 00:08:58.820
you might pass it into the API.

00:08:58.820 --> 00:09:00.680
And then, it will
automatically detect

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:03.770
what are the words
in that document,

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:06.540
and give you that
raw text afterwards.

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:09.500
Or maybe you've got
an image of a scene,

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:11.000
and there are signs on the wall.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:14.120
It'll give you the text
of what's on that sign.

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:17.956
So the OCR is really, really
handy in everyday applications.

00:09:17.956 --> 00:09:19.830
The adult content one
is actually one really,

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:21.288
really important,
because you might

00:09:21.288 --> 00:09:22.490
get user-submitted content.

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:24.099
You don't know
what's in that image.

00:09:24.099 --> 00:09:25.640
You need to make
sure that you're not

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:28.230
presenting an inappropriate
image to your users.

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:31.610
So the adult content
detection is a really easy way

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:34.050
for you to detect what's
going on in that image,

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:36.050
and is that something
that you should filter out

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:37.150
for your users?

00:09:37.150 --> 00:09:39.900
For the Conversation
class of APIs--

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:43.850
the audio to text-- you
might have a streaming audio

00:09:43.850 --> 00:09:47.001
file that you then need to
figure out what is being said.

00:09:47.001 --> 00:09:49.250
It will then transcribe that
event and give it to you.

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:53.420
It's useful for videos, for
real conversations with people,

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:55.700
for TV shows,
whatever it might be.

00:09:55.700 --> 00:09:57.129
The reverse is also possible.

00:09:57.129 --> 00:09:58.670
You might have a
text string that you

00:09:58.670 --> 00:10:00.330
need to read out to your user.

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:04.700
Well, you pass the string
to the application.

00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:06.710
And it will then create
the synthetic voice

00:10:06.710 --> 00:10:09.530
that you can then
read off to your user.

00:10:09.530 --> 00:10:12.770
Chat bots is a way for you
to engage with your users

00:10:12.770 --> 00:10:15.830
and program the interactions
you would like in chat directly

00:10:15.830 --> 00:10:18.770
with your user, and program
certain kinds of conversations

00:10:18.770 --> 00:10:21.350
to go on, and help them
with customer service calls

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:23.190
or things like that.

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:26.496
From the Language
APIs, one of the things

00:10:26.496 --> 00:10:28.620
that's really hard if if
you've got free-form text,

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:30.710
what the heck is
it really saying?

00:10:30.710 --> 00:10:34.050
We will map out and figure
out the context of what

00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:36.560
it's saying, map out the data
structure of the sentences,

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:38.400
give you the parts of speech
so you can figure out, OK,

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:39.860
what are the nouns,
what are the verbs, what

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:41.140
are the relationships of these?

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:42.890
And then, you can do
something intelligent

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:44.480
with that structure.

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:48.440
Sentiment analysis is
a situation where--

00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:51.350
what's the mood that's
being transmitted along

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:52.490
with those words?

00:10:52.490 --> 00:10:55.340
What we've had is-- we've seen
a lot of good success trying

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:59.750
to monitor, for instance, the
sentiment of customer service

00:10:59.750 --> 00:11:02.780
emails or chats, and when
things are getting out

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:05.100
of hand, being able to
escalate that to the managers

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:07.266
so you can kind of make
sure things don't spiral out

00:11:07.266 --> 00:11:08.030
of control.

00:11:08.030 --> 00:11:11.570
Or if you need to understand
if something's aggressive,

00:11:11.570 --> 00:11:14.499
or calm, or happy, how do you
make sure you're categorizing

00:11:14.499 --> 00:11:16.790
things appropriately if you're
like ratings up customer

00:11:16.790 --> 00:11:18.280
service calls, things like that?

00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:21.509
In translation, it's a great way
to just automatically translate

00:11:21.509 --> 00:11:22.800
from one language to the other.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:25.530
And we'll talk a little bit
about that next as an example.

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:28.530
So for instance, say you
are a game developer,

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:30.329
and you have an
online community,

00:11:30.329 --> 00:11:32.120
but they're coming from
all over the world,

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:33.080
speaking different languages.

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:35.038
You want them to be able
to talk to each other.

00:11:35.038 --> 00:11:36.620
In real-time chat,
you can basically

00:11:36.620 --> 00:11:40.740
have the ability to translate
to any of the other languages.

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:42.980
So the Translate API
makes that really easy.

00:11:42.980 --> 00:11:46.790
Or you might be able to get
inbound customer service

00:11:46.790 --> 00:11:49.460
emails, and be able to
translate that to a language

00:11:49.460 --> 00:11:51.770
that your customer service
rep understands so that they

00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:54.270
can then find the right article
to respond to your customer.

00:11:56.634 --> 00:11:58.800
The nice thing about these
APIs is they're basically

00:11:58.800 --> 00:11:59.582
